.*+?()[\\]{}|/]/g, '\\\\$&')\n\n// a RegExp that only matches `s`, with unicode case folding\nconst foldRe = (s: string) => new RegExp(`^${reEscape(s)}$`, 'iu')\n\n/**\n * A literal char. With `nocase:true`, `re` folds case, and `ascii`\n * holds its results for ASCII chars, by char code: 1 for a match,\n * and 2 for not.\n */\nexport type Lit = { ch: string; re?: RegExp; ascii?: Uint8Array }\n\nconst litMatch = (l: Lit, c: string) => {\n  if (c === l.ch) return true\n  if (!l.re) return false\n  const code = c.charCodeAt(0)\n  if (code >= 128) return l.re.test(c)\n  const ascii = (l.ascii ??= new Uint8Array(128))\n  ascii[code] ||= l.re.test(c) ? 1 : 2\n  return ascii[code] === 1\n}\n\nconst tokenize = (\n  p: string,\n  nocase: boolean,\n  noext: boolean,\n  form?: Form,\n) => {\n  const toks: Token[] = []\n  for (let i = 0; i < p.length;) {\n    const c = String.fromCodePoint(p.codePointAt(i)!)\n    if (c === '\\\\') {\n      // a trailing \\ is a literal \\\n      const e = p.codePointAt(i + 1)\n      const ch = e === undefined ? c : String.fromCodePoint(e)\n      toks.push(lit(ch))\n      i += 1 + ch.length\n      continue\n    }\n    if (c === '[') {\n      const [re, consumed] = parseClass(p, i, nocase, form)\n      if (consumed) {\n        toks.push({ t: 'class', re })\n        i += consumed\n        continue\n      }\n    }\n    if (!noext && extTypes.includes(c) && p[i + 1] === '(') {\n      toks.push({ t: 'open', type: c })\n      i += 2\n      continue\n    }\n    toks.push(\n      !noext && c === '|' ? { t: 'bar' }\n      : !noext && c === ')' ? { t: 'close' }\n      : c === '*' ? { t: 'star' }\n      : c === '?' ? { t: 'any' }\n      : lit(c),\n    )\n    i += c.length\n  }\n  return toks\n}\n\n// map each open token index to the index of its close token\nconst pair = (toks: Token[]) => {\n  const pairs = new Map<number, number>()\n  const stack: number[] = []\n  toks.forEach((tok, i) => {\n    if (tok.t === 'open') stack.push(i)\n    else if (tok.t === 'close' && stack.length) pairs.set(stack.pop()!, i)\n  })\n  return pairs\n}\n\n// build the alternatives between start and end. An open token that\n// is not closed is never inside a closed extglob, so bar and close\n// tokens are only literal at the top level.\nconst build = (\n  toks: Token[],\n  pairs: Map<number, number>,\n  start: number,\n  end: number,\n  top: boolean,\n): Item[][] => {\n  let seq: Item[] = []\n  const alts = [seq]\n  for (let i = start; i < end; i++) {\n    const tok = toks[i]!\n    const close = pairs.get(i)\n    if (tok.t === 'open' && close !== undefined) {\n      const sub = build(toks, pairs, i + 1, close, false)\n      seq.push({ t: 'ext', type: tok.type, alts: sub })\n      i = close\n    } else if (tok.t === 'bar' && !top) {\n      alts.push((seq = []))\n    } else if (tok.t === 'open') {\n      seq.push(lit(tok.type), lit('('))\n    } else if (tok.t === 'bar') {\n      seq.push(lit('|'))\n    } else if (tok.t === 'close') {\n      seq.push(lit(')'))\n    } else {\n      seq.push(tok)\n    }\n  }\n  return alts\n}\n\n// normalize each run of literal chars as one string, so that a char\n// and the combining marks after it compose (or decompose) together.\n// Syntax is already parsed, so a char that normalizes to a syntax\n// char (such as full-width `＊` in NFKC) stays a literal.\nconst normalize = (items: Item[], form: Form): Item[] => {\n  const out: Item[] = []\n  let run = ''\n  const flush = () => {\n    for (const ch of run.normalize(form)) out.push(lit(ch))\n    run = ''\n  }\n  for (const it of items) {\n    if (it.t === 'lit') {\n      run += it.ch\n      continue\n    }\n    flush()\n    out.push(\n      it.t === 'ext' ?\n        { ...it, alts: it.alts.map(a => normalize(a, form)) }\n      : it,\n    )\n  }\n  flush()\n  return out\n}\n\n// the start node, and the number of node ids. Ids are dense, from 0\n// for the match node.\nconst compile = (\n  items: Item[],\n  nocase: boolean,\n): [start: Node, size: number] => {\n  let id = 1\n  const split = (out: Node[]): SplitNode => ({ t: 'split', id: id++, out })\n  const seq = (items: Item[], next: Node): Node =>\n    items.reduceRight((next, it) => item(it, next), next)\n  const item = (it: Item, next: Node): Node => {\n    switch (it.t) {\n      case 'lit':\n        const re = nocase ? foldRe(it.ch) : undefined\n        return { t: 'lit', id: id++, ch: it.ch, re, next }\n      case 'any':\n      case 'star':\n        return { t: it.t, id: id++, next }\n      case 'class':\n        return { t: 'class', id: id++, re: it.re, next }\n    }\n    const alts = (next: Node) => it.alts.map(a => seq(a, next))\n    switch (it.type) {\n      case '@':\n        return split(alts(next))\n      case '?':\n        return split([next, ...alts(next)])\n      case '!':\n        return { t: 'neg', id: id++, sub: split(alts(MATCH)), next }\n    }\n    // * and +, loop back after each match\n    const after = split([])\n    const entry = split(alts(after))\n    after.out.push(next, entry)\n    return it.type === '*' ? after : entry\n  }\n  const start = seq(items, MATCH)\n  return [start, id]\n}\n\n// one line per node, in the order that a walk from the start\n// reaches them. Edges point to node ids. The nodes of a negated\n// sub-pattern are indented under their negation.\nconst show = (start: Node): string => {\n  const lines: string[] = []\n  const seen = new Set<Node>()\n  const ref = (n: Node) => (n.t === 'match' ? 'match' : String(n.id))\n  const walk = (n: Node, indent: string) => {\n    if (n.t === 'match' || seen.has(n)) return\n    seen.add(n)\n    const line = (desc: string) => lines.push(`${indent}${n.id}: ${desc}`)\n    switch (n.t) {\n      case 'split':\n        line(`split -> ${n.out.map(ref).join(', ')}`)\n        for (const o of n.out) walk(o, indent)\n        return\n      case 'neg':\n        line(`!(${ref(n.sub)}) -> ${ref(n.next)}`)\n        walk(n.sub, indent + '  ')\n        return walk(n.next, indent)\n      case 'lit':\n        line(`${JSON.stringify(n.ch)} -> ${ref(n.next)}`)\n        break\n      case 'class':\n        line(`${n.re} -> ${ref(n.next)}`)\n        break\n      default:\n        line(`${n.t === 'any' ? '?' : '*'} -> ${ref(n.next)}`)\n    }\n    walk(n.next, indent)\n  }\n  walk(start, '')\n  return [`start -> ${ref(start)}`, ...lines].join('\\n')\n}\n\n// A thread inside a !(...) negation. It holds the threads of the\n// negated sub-pattern, started where the negation started. The\n// negation matches the chars so far if the sub-pattern does not.\ntype Run = { t: 'run'; neg: NegNode; state: State }\n\n// The threads at one position in the string. Each node thread is\n// only kept once, and runs with the same state are only kept once,\n// so that identical threads merge. The match node is not in\n// `threads`, because it can not step. It sets `match` instead.\nclass State {\n  // set by the Sim, to mark the nodes that this state has\n  gen: number\n  threads: Node[] = []\n  match = false\n  runs: Run[] = []\n  // the sorted ids of the threads, with 0 for a match, once the\n  // state is done\n  ids: number[] = []\n\n  constructor(gen: number) {\n    this.gen = gen\n  }\n\n  // no threads, so nothing more can match\n  get dead() {\n    return !this.threads.length && !this.match && !this.runs.length\n  }\n}\n\n// true if the two states hold the same threads\nconst sameState = (a: State, b: State): boolean => {\n  if (a.ids.length !== b.ids.length) return false\n  if (a.runs.length !== b.runs.length) return false\n  if (a.ids.some((id, i) => id !== b.ids[i])) return false\n  return a.runs.every(r =>\n    b.runs.some(o => o.neg === r.neg && sameState(o.state, r.state)),\n  )\n}\n\n/**\n * For each node id, the gen of the last state that has the node as\n * a thread, and of the last state that walked through it. A state is\n * built to the end before the states around it go on, and the nodes\n * of a negated sub-pattern are only in its states (other than the\n * match node, which is never marked). So one mark for each node is\n * enough to know if the state being built has it. The Sims of a\n * matcher never run at the same time, so they share one Marks.\n */\nexport type Marks = { has: Float64Array; seen: Float64Array; gen: number }\n\nconst marks = (size: number): Marks => ({\n  has: new Float64Array(size),\n  seen: new Float64Array(size),\n  gen: 0,\n})\n\nclass Sim {\n  // `.` and `..` only match literal dots, even with `dot:true`\n  dots: boolean\n  // the string starts with `.`, and `dot:true` is not set\n  dotStart: boolean\n  #marks: Marks\n\n  constructor(dots: boolean, dotStart: boolean, marks: Marks) {\n    this.dots = dots\n    this.dotStart = dotStart\n    this.#marks = marks\n  }\n\n  state() {\n    return new State(++this.#marks.gen)\n  }\n\n  // sort the ids of a state, once it has all of its threads\n  done(state: State) {\n    state.ids = state.threads.map(n => n.id).sort((a, b) => a - b)\n    if (state.match) state.ids.unshift(MATCH.id)\n    return state\n  }\n\n  // true the first time that the state walks through the node, so\n  // that loops in the graph stop\n  #visit(state: State, node: Node) {\n    const { seen } = this.#marks\n    if (seen[node.id] === state.gen) return false\n    seen[node.id] = state.gen\n    return true\n  }\n\n  #addThread(state: State, node: Node) {\n    const { has } = this.#marks\n    if (has[node.id] === state.gen) return false\n    has[node.id] = state.gen\n    state.threads.push(node)\n    return true\n  }\n\n  // wildcards only match a leading dot if `dot:true`. In a negated\n  // sub-pattern, this does not apply, because the negation itself\n  // can not match a leading dot.\n  restricted(i: number, top: boolean) {\n    return top && (this.dots || (this.dotStart && i === 0))\n  }\n\n  add(state: State, node: Node, i: number, top: boolean): void {\n    switch (node.t) {\n      case 'split':\n        if (!this.#visit(state, node)) return\n        for (const o of node.out) this.add(state, o, i, top)\n        return\n      case 'neg':\n        if (!this.#visit(state, node)) return\n        const sub = this.state()\n        this.add(sub, node.sub, i, false)\n        this.done(sub)\n        return this.addRun(\n          state,\n          { t: 'run', neg: node, state: sub },\n          i,\n          top,\n        )\n      case 'star':\n        // a * can not start a dotfile, even if it matches nothing\n        if (this.restricted(i, top)) return\n        if (this.#addThread(state, node))\n          this.add(state, node.next, i, top)\n        return\n      case 'match':\n        state.match = true\n        return\n      default:\n        this.#addThread(state, node)\n    }\n  }\n\n  addRun(state: State, run: Run, i: number, top: boolean) {\n    for (const r of state.runs) {\n      if (r.neg === run.neg && sameState(r.state, run.state)) return\n    }\n    state.runs.push(run)\n    if (!run.state.match) {\n      this.add(state, run.neg.next, i, top)\n    }\n  }\n\n  // the threads after char `c`, at index `i`\n  step(state: State, c: string, i: number, top: boolean): State {\n    const next = this.state()\n    for (const th of state.threads) {\n      switch (th.t) {\n        case 'lit':\n          if (litMatch(th, c)) {\n            this.add(next, th.next, i + 1, top)\n          }\n          break\n        case 'any':\n          if (!this.restricted(i, top)) this.add(next, th.next, i + 1, top)\n          break\n        case 'class':\n          if (!this.restricted(i, top) && th.re.test(c)) {\n            this.add(next, th.next, i + 1, top)\n          }\n          break\n        case 'star':\n          if (!this.restricted(i, top)) this.add(next, th, i + 1, top)\n      }\n    }\n    if (!this.restricted(i, top)) {\n      for (const run of state.runs) {\n        // a sub-pattern with no threads can not match again, so the\n        // run stays the same\n        if (run.state.dead) {\n          this.addRun(next, run, i + 1, top)\n          continue\n        }\n        const sub = this.step(run.state, c, i, false)\n        this.addRun(\n          next,\n          { t: 'run', neg: run.neg, state: sub },\n          i + 1,\n          top,\n        )\n      }\n    }\n    return this.done(next)\n  }\n}\n\n// A canonical key for the threads in a state. States with the same\n// key hold the same threads, so they match the same strings.\nconst stateKey = (state: State): string => {\n  const ids = state.ids.join()\n  if (!state.runs.length) return ids\n  const runs = state.runs.map(r => `${r.neg.id}(${stateKey(r.state)})`)\n  return `${ids}|${runs.sort().join('|')}`\n}\n\n// A cached state of the simulation, and the states that each char\n// leads to.\ntype DState = {\n  state: State\n  match: boolean\n  // no threads, so nothing more can match\n  dead: boolean\n  // the next state for each ASCII char, by char code, and for the\n  // other chars\n  ascii?: (DState | undefined)[]\n  next: Map<string, DState>\n}\n\n// The cache can hold about this many chars of state keys and\n// transitions. When it is full, it is cleared, so that a pattern\n// can not use an unbounded amount of memory.\nconst DFA_BUDGET = 1 << 18\n\n// A cache of the states that the simulation reaches, built as the\n// matcher is used. Each char of a string is then one Map lookup,\n// once the states that it leads to are known.\n//\n// The dot rules only restrict the first char of a dotfile (strings\n// that are only `.` or `..` do not use the cache). So a dotfile\n// starts at its own state, which takes its first step with the\n// restriction, and every other state takes steps without it.\nclass Dfa {\n  #start: Node\n  #states = new Map<string, DState>()\n  #size = 0\n  #plain?: DState\n  #dotStart?: DState\n  #sim: Sim\n  #dotSim: Sim\n\n  constructor(start: Node, marks: Marks) {\n    this.#start = start\n    this.#sim = new Sim(false, false, marks)\n    this.#dotSim = new Sim(false, true, marks)\n  }\n\n  #make(state: State): DState {\n    return {\n      state,\n      match: state.match,\n      dead: state.dead,\n      next: new Map(),\n    }\n  }\n\n  #intern(state: State): DState {\n    const key = stateKey(state)\n    const found = this.#states.get(key)\n    if (found) return found\n    if (this.#size > DFA_BUDGET) this.#clear()\n    const d = this.#make(state)\n    this.#states.set(key, d)\n    this.#size += key.length + 1\n    return d\n  }\n\n  #clear() {\n    this.#states.clear()\n    this.#size = 0\n    this.#plain = undefined\n    this.#dotStart = undefined\n  }\n\n  start(dotStart: boolean): DState {\n    if (dotStart) {\n      if (this.#dotStart) return this.#dotStart\n      const state = this.#dotSim.state()\n      this.#dotSim.add(state, this.#start, 0, true)\n      return (this.#dotStart = this.#make(this.#dotSim.done(state)))\n    }\n    if (this.#plain) return this.#plain\n    const state = this.#sim.state()\n    this.#sim.add(state, this.#start, 0, true)\n    return (this.#plain = this.#intern(this.#sim.done(state)))\n  }\n\n  // the next state, after a miss in `d.ascii`\n  next(d: DState, c: string): DState {\n    const code = c.charCodeAt(0)\n    const found = code < 128 ? undefined : d.next.get(c)\n    if (found) return found\n    // the dotfile start is not in the cache, so it is never shared\n    const n =\n      d === this.#dotStart ?\n        this.#intern(this.#dotSim.step(d.state, c, 0, true))\n      : this.#intern(this.#sim.step(d.state, c, 1, true))\n    if (code < 128) {\n      if (!d.ascii) {\n        d.ascii = new Array(128)\n        this.#size += 128\n      }\n      d.ascii[code] = n\n    } else {\n      d.next.set(c, n)\n    }\n    this.#size++\n    return n\n  }\n}\n\n/**\n * A node in a graph with no splits or negations. Such a graph is a\n * chain of nodes that each match one char, or a `*`.\n */\nexport type LinearNode = Exclude<\n  Node,\n  SplitNode | NegNode | { t: 'match' }\n>\n\n// the nodes in order, if the graph is a single chain\nconst linear = (start: Node): LinearNode[] | undefined => {\n  const nodes: LinearNode[] = []\n  for (let n = start; n.t !== 'match'; n = n.next) {\n    if (n.t === 'split' || n.t === 'neg') return undefined\n    nodes.push(n)\n  }\n  return nodes\n}\n\n// Match a chain of nodes. Each node other than `*` matches exactly\n// one char, so on a failure it is enough to go back to the last `*`\n// and let it match one more char. This takes at most\n// nodes.length * chars.length steps.\nconst testLinear = (\n  nodes: LinearNode[],\n  chars: ArrayLike<string>,\n  start: number,\n  end: number,\n) => {\n  let ni = 0\n  let ci = start\n  let starNi = -1\n  let starCi = 0\n  while (ci < end) {\n    const n = nodes[ni]\n    if (n?.t === 'star') {\n      starNi = ni++\n      starCi = ci\n      continue\n    }\n    const c = chars[ci]!\n    if (\n      n &&\n      (n.t === 'any' ||\n        (n.t === 'lit' && litMatch(n, c)) ||\n        (n.t === 'class' && n.re.test(c)))\n    ) {\n      ni++\n      ci++\n      continue\n    }\n    if (starNi === -1) return false\n    ni = starNi + 1\n    ci = ++starCi\n  }\n  while (nodes[ni]?.t === 'star') ni++\n  return ni === nodes.length\n}\n\n// The least and most chars that the items can match. These are only\n// bounds: a negation can match a string of any length, so it counts\n// as 0 to Infinity.\nconst lengths = (items: Item[]): [min: number, max: number] => {\n  let min = 0\n  let max = 0\n  for (const it of items) {\n    const [lo, hi] = itemLengths(it)\n    min += lo\n    max += hi\n  }\n  return [min, max]\n}\n\nconst itemLengths = (it: Item): [min: number, max: number] => {\n  if (it.t === 'star') return [0, Infinity]\n  if (it.t !== 'ext') return [1, 1]\n  if (it.type === '!') return [0, Infinity]\n  const alts = it.alts.map(lengths)\n  const min = Math.min(...alts.map(a => a[0]))\n  const max = Math.max(...alts.map(a => a[1]))\n  // a loop of alternatives that only match the empty string is empty\n  const loop = max === 0 ? 0 : Infinity\n  return (\n    it.type === '@' ? [mi